\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
NIST Processing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strictly,
 from NIST’s own documentation,
 the safest reconstruction is the following.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST says that the raw Alice and Bob files are time-tagged event streams.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each raw record contains a channel number,
 a raw time tag and a transfer counter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time-tag resolution is 78.125 ps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alice and Bob’s time taggers share the same 10 MHz reference so they run at the same rate,
 although their absolute time origins differ.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The stored raw-data channels are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0 detector click
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2 RNG output 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
4 RNG output 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
5 GPS PPS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
6 sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST explicitly describes channel 6 as the arrival of a sync pulse used to turn on the Pockels cell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is the output of the interface circuit that samples the random-number generator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From there,
 the NIST processing chain is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
raw Alice event stream
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
raw Bob event stream
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
↓
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
identify sync events
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
identify RNG setting associated with each sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
identify detector clicks relative to each sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
↓
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
construct one processed "event"
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
↓
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
event contains:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
16-bit Alice click mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
16-bit Bob click mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last step is not an interpretation:
 NIST explicitly says that its processed cw45 format is a time-ordered record of events with each event containing the settings plus two 16-bit click fields.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each processed event the setting byte is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
bit 0 :
 Alice RNG setting 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
bit 1 :
 Alice RNG setting 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
bit 2 :
 Bob RNG setting 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
bit 3 :
 Bob RNG setting 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST notes that on rare occasions both or neither setting bits may be present for one party so an analysis should not silently assume every event has exactly one valid setting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The click representation is equally explicit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alice gets a 16-bit unsigned integer and Bob gets another 16-bit unsigned integer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST says that there are 16 possible laser cycles while the Pockels cell is on and bit position 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 indicates whether or not a click occurred in that laser slot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
A_{k} & = & \sum_{j=0}^{15}a_{kj}2^{j}\\
B_{k} & = & \sum_{j=0}^{15}b_{kj}2^{j}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $a_{kj},b_{kj}\in\{0,1\}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Slot 1 corresponds to bit 0 and slot 16 to bit 15.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So,
 strictly following NIST,
 a processing algorithm can be written as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
READ Alice raw records
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
READ Bob raw records
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
for each side:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
extract channel-6 sync timestamps
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
extract channel-2 and channel-4 RNG timestamps
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
extract channel-0 detector timestamps
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
align Alice and Bob streams in time
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
for each valid sync/event k:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
determine Alice RNG setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
determine Bob RNG setting
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
for each detector click near that sync:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
compute detector delay relative to sync
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
determine which of the 16 laser slots that the click belongs to
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
set corresponding bit in Alice or Bob click mask
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
save:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Alice_click_mask[16]
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bob_click_mask[16]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The critical detail is that the click window is defined relative to the sync.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST says that its HDF5 files store the relative delay from the sync used to define a click,
 the radius of the timing window,
 each detector click’s delay relative to sync and the sync number associated with each detector click.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 for a given event 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and pulse slot 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 the local result is simply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
A_{kj} & = & \begin{cases}
1, & \text{Alice click-mask bit }j=1,\\
0, & \text{Alice click-mask bit }j=0,
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

and similarly:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
B_{kj} & = & \begin{cases}
1, & \text{Bob click-mask bit }j=1,\\
0, & \text{Bob click-mask bit }j=0.
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Hence the four outcomes for that pulse slot are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
++ & \equiv & (1,1),\\
+0 & \equiv & (1,0),\\
0+ & \equiv & (0,1),\\
00 & \equiv & (0,0).
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This terminology is confirmed by NIST’s own spreadsheet description:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
the processed analysis files have columns ordered as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
++ab,\quad+0ab,\quad0+ab,\quad00ab,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and then the same four outcomes for 
\begin_inset Formula $ab'$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a'b$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a'b'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 00 is reconstructed,
 not detected.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a valid event with a valid Alice/Bob setting pair examine a specified pulse slot or pulse grouping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If neither Alice nor Bob has a click bit set for that slot/group then the outcome is 00.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In pseudocode:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
for each processed event k:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Alice setting is invalid:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
reject / flag event
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Bob setting is invalid:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
reject / flag event
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
for each pulse slot j of interest:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
A = bit (Alice_click_mask,
 j)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
B = bit (Bob_click_mask,
 j)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if A == 1 and B == 1:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
outcome = "++"
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
elif A == 1 and B == 0:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
outcome = "+0"
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
elif A == 0 and B == 1:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
outcome = "0+"
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
else:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout LyX-Code
outcome = "00"
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
There is one major qualification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST explicitly warns that the old processed_compressed/cw45 files do not contain enough metadata to reproduce the full event definition.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The name cw45 only tells you that a radius of 4 was used at Alice and 5 at Bob;
 the actual delay from the sync pulse has to come from the YAML configuration used by build_file_txt.py.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST actually discourages relying on those files alone for analysis because the full chain is not documented there.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The HDF5 form is therefore much better for a reconstruction because it preserves the settings,
 click masks,
 anomalous syncs,
 clock-alignment information,
 click-window delay and radius,
 individual click delays relative to sync and the sync number for each detector click.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One more important warning from NIST:
 some of Peter Bierhorst’s spreadsheets have incorrect 00 entries due to a programming error.
 However NIST says those entries did not affect the reported p-value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So for your own clean analysis,
 I would define the core data object as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boxed{(k,\;x_{k},\;y_{k},\;A_{k}^{(16)},\;B_{k}^{(16)})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is the synchronized event number,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k},y_{k}$
\end_inset

 are the RNG settings,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}^{(16)},B_{k}^{(16)}$
\end_inset

 are the 16-bit click masks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then 00 is not found by searching for a special record.
 It is simply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boxed{a_{kj}=0,\qquad b_{kj}=0}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for a valid synchronized event and the pulse slot or pulse grouping being analysed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next useful step is to go one level deeper into the NIST analysis code—
especially build_file_txt.py / the HDF5-building code—
and extract the exact rule by which a detector delay is assigned to one of those 16 bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That would remove the last ambiguity between the sync histogram you already produced and the final ++,+0,0+,00 records.
\end_layout

